From zaslavsk at hcp.med.harvard.edu  Tue Feb  6 17:20:07 2001
From: zaslavsk at hcp.med.harvard.edu (Alan Zaslavsky)
Date: Tue, 06 Feb 2001 12:20:07 -0500
Subject: Re: [S] How to keep Splus going after a failure of inverting a matrix
In-Reply-To: <f9440ed23a4957a2839ebe95224ae27e@NO-ID-FOUND.mhonarc.org>
Message-ID: <b952ea03232477640a4204b308f3a094@NO-ID-FOUND.mhonarc.org>

There are a couple of ways to explicitly check whether the matrix is
singular. You could use qr() and see if the rank component indicates that
the matrix is less than full rank.  If you look at the code for solve()
you will see that it uses qr() so adding that check is easy and adds no
calculations.

> solve.default
function(a, b)
{
    if(!(missing(b) || is.numeric(b) || mode(b) == "complex"))
        stop("b must be numeric or complex")
    if(!is.list(a))
        a <- qr(a)
    if(missing(b))
        solve.qr(a)
    else solve.qr(a, b)
}

You also could use svd() (or eigen(), if the matrix is symmetric) and set
a criterion (e.g. ratio of largest to smallest singular value < 1e8) that
defines the matrix as almost singular.

